  "publication_binding_sha256": "72b16f1d88d63f5929f69196043079c3d7bcf3bd31a29f36409577a56406c6f3",
  "candidate_publication_context": {
    "file": "035e3b358baff4792ed2bef536b065847f27625d607c0861dd3e92a6fbbe4772",
    "current_lean_module": "import Mathlib.Data.Real.Basic\nimport Mathlib.Tactic.Linarith\n\n/-!\n# Recursive RGO condition-number progress\n\nSource: Chen, Chewi, Lu and Zhang, *Smoothed Picard Hamiltonian Monte Carlo*,\narXiv:2609.06906v1, Lemma 6.6(i), equations (6.1)--(6.3).\n\nAfter substituting `tau = k` and `a * beta_A = h + tau`, the next condition\nnumber is `k * (k + h + 1) / (2 * k + h)`. This file proves the scalar\ncontraction used to bound the number of ill-conditioned recursion stages.\nIdentifying this expression with an actual RGO condition number remains a\nseparate source-facing adapter; neither an RGO nor a sampling law is defined\nhere. Closed endpoints for `h` are harmless at this arithmetic layer only.\n-/\n\nnamespace AutoSamplingTheory.ExampleCases.SmoothedPicardHMC.RecursiveCondition\n\n/-- The ill-conditioned recursive update lies between half and four fifths\nof its previous condition number. The denominator is proved positive from\nthe hypotheses, so no totalized-division exceptional case is used. -/\ntheorem contraction_bounds {k h : ℝ} (hk : 2 ≤ k) (hh : 0 ≤ h)\n    (hh_upper : h ≤ 1 / 4) :\n    k / 2 ≤ k * (k + h + 1) / (2 * k + h) ∧\n      k * (k + h + 1) / (2 * k + h) ≤ (4 / 5) * k := by\n  have hk_nonneg : 0 ≤ k := by linarith\n  have hden : 0 < 2 * k + h := by linarith\n  constructor\n  · apply (le_div_iff₀ hden).2\n    nlinarith [mul_nonneg hk_nonneg hh]\n  · apply (div_le_iff₀ hden).2\n    have hmargin : 0 ≤ 3 * k - h - 5 := by linarith\n    nlinarith [mul_nonneg hk_nonneg hmargin]\n\nend AutoSamplingTheory.ExampleCases.SmoothedPicardHMC.RecursiveCondition\n",
    "toolchain": "302cd63c54178885b89e669f33b38f12f4dd7ae7e5cac537b3203e3768d8fb2b",
    "dependencies": "b83ca83b9cf7caa85fa8b023c2ff9a3fb7a7cce7e1c332e810c348535ec49c37",
    "source": {
      "url": "https://arxiv.org/html/2609.06906v1#S6.SS3",
      "edition": "arXiv:2609.06906v1",
      "anchor": "§6.3, Lemma 6.6(i), (6.3), from (6.1)–(6.2)",
      "wording_status": "faithful paraphrase",
      "attribution": "Fan Chen, Sinho Chewi, Jianfeng Lu and Matthew S. Zhang. ASTIS mathematical restatement, not original prose; no endorsement implied."
    },
    "statement": "In the recursive RGO scheme let c0 be in (0,1/4), with 0<eta_j≤c0 at each stage. If the current ratio kappa_j of specified curvature bounds is at least 2, the next ratio is between one half and four fifths of the current ratio.",
    "formulae": [
      {
        "label": "(6.3)",
        "tex": "\\frac12\\kappa_j\\le\\kappa_{j+1}\\le\\frac45\\kappa_j."
      },
      {
        "label": "substitution of (6.1)–(6.2)",
        "tex": "\\kappa_{j+1}=\\frac{\\kappa_j(\\kappa_j+\\eta_j+1)}{2\\kappa_j+\\eta_j}."
      }
    ],
    "assumptions": [
      "The recursive parameters follow (6.1): kappa_next=kappa*(a*beta_A+1)/(a*beta_A+kappa), with beta_A>0.",
      "The ill-conditioned branch of (6.2) sets tau=kappa, a=(eta+tau)/beta_A; kappa≥2 and 0<eta≤c0<1/4.",
      "The ratio comes from the RGO curvature parameters in Lemma 6.4; it is not an independently assumed mixing rate."
    ],
    "obligations": [
      {
        "id": "scalar-progress",
        "label": "Positive-denominator rational calculation with the source constants"
      },
      {
        "id": "rgo-parameter-identification",
        "label": "Actual recursive RGO curvature ratios satisfy (6.1)–(6.2), including A=infinity"
      }
    ],
    "lesson": {
      "declaration": "AutoSamplingTheory.ExampleCases.SmoothedPicardHMC.RecursiveCondition.contraction_bounds",
      "kind": "theorem",
      "title": "Why an ill-conditioned recursive stage makes geometric progress",
      "statement": "For any real numbers k and h with k at least 2 and h between 0 and 1/4, the displayed rational update lies between k/2 and four fifths of k.",
      "formula": "k_+=\\frac{k(k+h+1)}{2k+h},\\qquad \\frac{k}{2}\\le k_+\\le\\frac45 k.",
      "assumptions": [
        "k,h are real scalars; 2 ≤ k and 0 ≤ h ≤ 1/4.",
        "In the paper application k is the ratio of the specified curvature bounds, h is the smoothing parameter, and tau=k. It is not necessarily the optimal condition number of the target.",
        "The original range 0<h≤c0<1/4 is contained in this closed scalar domain. No claim extends the sampler itself to zero smoothing.",
        "This calculation does not involve a measure, derivative or random variable. The recurrence-to-RGO identification is a separate obligation."
      ],
      "steps": [
        {
          "title": "Check division is legitimate",
          "text": "The denominator is positive. Thus multiplying either inequality by it preserves its direction; Lean's exceptional value for division by zero is irrelevant.",
          "formula": "D=2k+h\\ge4>0.",
          "lean": "hden proves strict positivity; le_div_iff₀ and div_le_iff₀ remove the denominator only after this proof."
        },
        {
          "title": "Prove the lower bound",
          "text": "Subtract half the previous value. The numerator is nonnegative because both k and h are nonnegative.",
          "formula": "k_+-\\frac{k}{2}=\\frac{k(h+2)}{2D}\\ge0.",
          "lean": "The first branch uses mul_nonneg hk_nonneg hh, then nlinarith checks the cleared-denominator polynomial inequality."
        },
        {
          "title": "Prove the uniform contraction",
          "text": "The deficit from four fifths has a positive margin throughout the parameter range. This checks the paper's constants without an unspecified further reduction of c0.",
          "formula": "\\frac45k-k_+=\\frac{k(3k-h-5)}{5D},\\qquad 3k-h-5\\ge6-\\frac14-5=\\frac34>0.",
          "lean": "hmargin proves 0 ≤ 3*k-h-5 by linarith. Its product with k is nonnegative; nlinarith closes the upper bound."
        }
      ],
      "lean_statement": "The parameters in braces are implicit real numbers; hk, hh and hh_upper are explicit hypotheses. The conjunction returns both inequalities. No typeclass or unproved sampler property is hidden in the statement.",
      "lean_proof": "constructor separates the two inequalities. Positivity licenses cross-multiplication; mul_nonneg supplies the needed products; linarith and nlinarith generate checked ordered-field proofs. ASTIS authors this source-specific recurrence estimate; Mathlib supplies real arithmetic and the proof-producing tactics.",
      "astis_dependencies": [],
      "mathlib_dependencies": [
        "le_div_iff₀ and div_le_iff₀: ordered-field division with a strictly positive denominator.",
        "mul_nonneg: nonnegative products; Mathlib linarith/nlinarith: kernel-checked polynomial arithmetic."
      ],
      "sources": [
        {
          "url": "https://arxiv.org/html/2609.06906v1#S6.SS3",
          "label": "Chen, Chewi, Lu and Zhang, Smoothed Picard Hamiltonian Monte Carlo v1",
          "scope": "Lemma 6.6(i), (6.3), after substituting (6.1) and (6.2). ASTIS exposition, not quoted prose."
        }
      ],
